<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Mancha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Mancha is a high plateau region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a> in 1195, Almohad forces raided through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> valley and La Mancha up to the walls of Toledo. The region was a frontier zone between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-mancha.jpg" alt="La Mancha" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lourdes Cardenal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campo_de_Criptana_Molinos_de_Viento_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>La Mancha is a natural region and comarca in the autonomous community of Castilla-La Mancha, covering parts of the provinces of Albacete, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-real">Ciudad Real</a>, Cuenca and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. Authors give its area as anywhere from 15,000 to 30,000 square kilometres, and its boundaries are imprecise. It is a vast plain at an average altitude of 600 to 700 metres, formed by Miocene sediments of limestone and marl, and it lies across the basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Júcar</a> rivers. The climate is continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, with harsh winters, hot summers and annual rainfall of 300 to 400 millimetres. Its name is often thought to be Arabic, from a word for &quot;high plateau&quot;, though other derivations have been proposed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> remains are plentiful, especially along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> and its tributaries. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> declined in the fifth century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and Alans passed through, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> their capital in 569; vast areas stayed depopulated. After the Arab conquest of 711 the region remained sparsely populated under Muslim rule, though Calatrava, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, Cuenca and Alcaraz grew, the last two as centres of the textile industry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> contributed advanced irrigation techniques and introduced Merino sheep.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> became a hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-comuneros">Revolt of the Comuneros</a> of 1520, which ended in defeat in 1522; in 1523 Pope Adrian VI attached the masterships of the military orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and Calatrava to the Spanish Crown. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> of 1808 to 1813 devastated the region, and the Superior Junta of La Mancha, formed against the Afrancesado administration, published a gazette in 1811 and 1812. The prefectures made in 1810, with Manzanares as capital, did not outlast the return of Ferdinand VII in 1814.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>La Mancha has traditionally been agrarian, with dryland cereals, grapes and olives and transhumant sheep. Its vineyards are the largest in the world by area, and the La Mancha wine denomination covers 190,980 hectares. Saffron has its own protected designation. Wetlands such as the Tablas de Daimiel National Park and the Lagunas de Ruidera belong to the Mancha Húmeda, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve. The region is the setting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a>'s novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote">Don Quixote</a>, published in 1605 and 1615, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a> and characters have become symbols of La Mancha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Mancha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The victory earned him the honorific al-Mansur, &quot;the Victorious&quot;, and in the following campaigns his forces devastated Extremadura, the Tagus valley and <b>La Mancha</b>, raiding to the walls of Toledo in the deepest Muslim penetration of Christian territory for generations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>To defend the exposed frontier the Order of Calatrava was founded in 1158, and the Order of Santiago, founded in León in 1170, soon settled at Uclés; these military orders held castles and large estates across <b>La Mancha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Alonso Quixano is an hidalgo nearing 50 who lives in an unspecified region of <b>La Mancha</b> with his niece and housekeeper.</blockquote><a href="/work/don-quixote">Don Quixote · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
